\date{2026-04-24T11:18:38Z}
\tag{discrete}
\taxon{Example}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{Solve for #{x}: ##{523x \equiv_{17405} 77.}  We need to cancel the #{523}, so we can run the Extended Euclidean Algorithm on #{523} and #{17405}, both to make sure that the GCD is #{1}, and if so, to find the inverse of #{523}.}
##{\begin{array}{ccc}
  s & t & 523s + 17405t & q \\ \hline
  0 & 1 & 17405 & \\
  1 & 0 & 523 & 33 \\
  -33 & 1 & 146 & 3 \\
  100 & -3 & 85 & 1 \\
  -133 & 4 & 61 & 1 \\
  233 & -7 & 24 & 2 \\
  -599 & 18 & 13 & 1 \\
  832 & -25 & 11 & 1 \\
  -1431 & 43 & 2 & 5 \\
  7987 & -240 & 1 &  \\
\end{array}}
\p{Sure enough, the GCD is #{1}, and #{7987} is the inverse of #{523} modulo #{17405} (it's the value of #{s} we want, since that is the one being multiplied by #{523}).  Therefore, multiplying both sides of the equivalence by #{7987} will cancel the #{523} and allow us to solve for #{x}: ##{x \equiv_{17405} 77 \cdot 7987 = 614999 \equiv_{17405} 5824.}}
